Andrea

I'm mildly confused about how this post from 2006 came to be part of this message; but I'll take things at face value and assume you want to calculate effect size and are wondering whether the Z-statistic shown in eqn 3.102 of Buckland et al. (2001:85) is appropriate.  The answer is that this calculation is appropriate, unless degrees of freedom (shown in eqn 3.101) is small, i.e. <30.  This degrees of freedom calculation is a combination of uncertainties in the two density estimates.

If you look at a reasonable single data set, the Montrave robin (available at http://synergy.st-andrews.ac.uk/ds-manda/#montrave-songbird-case-study) line transect data (19 transects with 20 detections in total), there were 95 degrees of freedom associated with that data set; implying that this combined degrees of freedom > 30 is not a difficult hurdle to surpass.

If you don't have access to Buckland et al. (2001), I'll type the equation for you, but it would be quicker if you could find a copy of the text.

